<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100" phx-no-curly-interpolation>
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-nono"
      updated="September 9, 2026"
      evidence="The sandboxed MCP transport and an audited action were live-tested with nono 0.75.0 on macOS on September 7, 2026; a signed-in agent was not part of that test."
      toc={[
        {"start", "Start the connection"},
        {"install", "Install nono and the bridge"},
        {"configure", "Configure your agent"},
        {"run", "Run the agent in nono"},
        {"verify", "Verify the connection"},
        {"boundary", "Know the boundary"},
        {"rotation", "Rotate the key"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-nono">
        <:dek>
          Run a local CLI agent under nono's operating-system sandbox and extend that control to
          infrastructure and third-party tools with emisar.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        nono limits the files, environment variables, commands, and network destinations available
        to a local process. emisar separately limits the infrastructure actions that process can
        request. This guide uses nono's maintained agent profiles and adds emisar to the network
        allowlist.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          A macOS or Linux computer and a CLI agent available in nono's <.link
            href="https://registry.nono.sh"
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >profile registry</.link>.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          The agent installed and signed in. Keep its provider credentials in the location its
          nono profile expects; do not copy them into the project.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          An emisar account with permission to connect an agent and at least one runner online.
          Complete the
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >quickstart</.link>
          first if you do not have a runner yet.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          GitHub CLI installed so the emisar bridge installer can verify its release attestation.
          A GitHub login is not required.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="start">1. Start the connection</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a
          href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Connect an agent</a>, choose the CLI agent you will run under nono, select your host
        operating system, and expand <strong class="text-zinc-200">Set up manually</strong>.
        Keep the page open while you install the bridge and save the generated configuration.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">2. Install nono and the bridge</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Install nono with its public installer, then install the emisar bridge without its
        interactive client setup. You will use the client-specific configuration from step 1.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="host shell"
        copy_text="curl -fsSL https://nono.sh/install.sh | sh\ncurl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh -o /tmp/install-mcp.sh\nsudo bash /tmp/install-mcp.sh --yes\nnono --version\nemisar-mcp --version"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://nono.sh/install.sh | sh
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh -o /tmp/install-mcp.sh
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo bash /tmp/install-mcp.sh --yes
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> nono --version
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> emisar-mcp --version</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Homebrew users can install nono with <.docs_inline_code>brew install nono</.docs_inline_code>.
        Other package-manager options are in nono's <.link
          href="https://github.com/nolabs-ai/nono#quickstart"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >
          installation guide
        </.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="configure">3. Configure your agent</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Copy the generated emisar entry from the console into the client configuration path shown
        there. Merge it with existing settings and MCP servers. Keep that file outside the project,
        readable only by your user; it contains the reveal-once emisar key.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Use the bridge, not direct HTTP">
        <p>
          The tested path runs
          <.docs_inline_code>emisar-mcp</.docs_inline_code>
          as a child of the sandboxed agent. A direct HTTPS MCP configuration has different
          credential storage and rotation behavior and is not this walkthrough.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="run">4. Run the agent in nono</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        From the project directory, find the maintained profile and run the agent with
        <.docs_inline_code>emisar.dev</.docs_inline_code>
        added to its network allowlist. This example uses Codex; replace both
        <.docs_inline_code>codex</.docs_inline_code>
        values with your agent's registry name and executable.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="host shell"
        copy_text="nono search codex\nnono run --profile nolabs-ai/codex --allow-domain emisar.dev -- codex"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> nono search codex
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> nono run --profile nolabs-ai/codex --allow-domain emisar.dev -- codex</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Review the profile before accepting any suggested expansion. The working directory is where
        the agent is expected to read and write; a request for your whole home directory, SSH keys,
        cloud credentials, or container socket changes the boundary materially.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">5. Verify the connection</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>Use your agent's MCP status command and confirm that emisar is connected.</li>
        <li>
          Ask: <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Use emisar to find a runner with
          linux.uptime, run it, and show me the output.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          Complete any approval required by your <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >emisar policy</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>
          and confirm the action, runner, operator, and result.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="boundary">Know the boundary</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          nono enforces the profile you run. It does not hide files or permit network destinations
          that the profile itself allows, including files in the working directory.
        </li>
        <li>
          The agent can use the emisar key in its own MCP configuration. That key reaches only the
          runners and actions allowed to its owner, and every accepted action is audited.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep emisar policy and approval rules in place even if the agent locally trusts or
          auto-approves the emisar MCP server.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="rotation">Rotate the key</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The strict macOS profile used for qualification made the bridge's credential state
        read-only, so automatic key rotation was disabled. Treat rotation as manual in this setup:
        rotate the key from <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>, replace it in the private client configuration, and start a fresh nono
        session. Do not grant the sandbox an entire credential directory just to enable automatic
        rotation.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
